  "Number": 43,
  "Question": "\\(\\overline{AB}\\) is the hypotenuse of a right triangle \\(ABC.\\) Median \\(AD=7\\) and median \\(BE=4.\\) The length of \\(AB\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10\\)",
    "B": "\\(5\\sqrt3\\)",
    "C": "\\(5\\sqrt2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\sqrt{13}\\)",
    "E": "\\(2\\sqrt{15}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Because \\(AB\\) is the hypotenuse, the right angle is at \\(C\\). Let \\(AC=a\\) and \\(BC=b\\). A median ends at the midpoint of the opposite side, so\n\\[\nCD=\\frac b2,\\qquad CE=\\frac a2.\n\\]\nThe triangles \\(ACD\\) and \\(BCE\\) are both right at \\(C\\). Applying the Pythagorean theorem to their median hypotenuses gives\n\\[\na^2+\\frac{b^2}{4}=7^2=49,\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2+\\frac{a^2}{4}=4^2=16.\n\\]\nWe need \\(a^2+b^2\\), since that equals \\(AB^2\\). Adding the equations produces exactly this sum:\n\\[\n\\frac54(a^2+b^2)=65,\n\\qquad a^2+b^2=52.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(AB^2=52\\). Taking the positive square root because \\(AB\\) is a length,\n\\[\nAB=\\sqrt{52}=\\boxed{2\\sqrt{13}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
